  "live_records": {
    "q-104053": {
      "dom_id": "step-q104053",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14243229/assets/b8131b2ca68e378ef96c1b57e655db0a89d6e5207f0ac842612c64b30a963912.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $1,$ $2$ and $5,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,5]$ given that $f(1)=-12.$",
      "worked_solution": "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=\\pm 1.$ Therefore, the candidates are  $x=-2,-1,1,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =-12 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =f(1)-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-12-(-2) \\\\  & =-12+2 \\\\  & =-10 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{-2}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =f(1)-{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-12-(-2+1) \\\\  & =-12+1 \\\\  & =-11 \\\\ f(5) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-12-5 \\\\  & =-17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-12,-10,-11,-17\\}=-10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is  ${f}_{\\text{max}}=-10,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
