    "q-344169": {
      "dom_id": "step-q344169",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14243229/assets/3a971f2e8581ad9a623a8c8541ee1fcae5911fa0d3ae1b91923d19612d74ec9c.png)\n\nThe function $s(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-7,-1].$ The graph of ${s}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $s(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $14.$\n\nGiven that $s(-1)=3,$ what is the minimum value ${s}_{\\text{min}}$ of $s(x)$ on the interval $[-7,-1]?$\n\n${s}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $s(x)$ on $[-7,-1]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${s}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-7,-1),$ and ${s}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-7,-1.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $s$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s(-1) & =3 \\\\ s(-7) & =s(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{-7}{s}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =s(-1)-{\\int }_{-7}^{-1}{s}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3-(-14) \\\\  & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $s$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $s$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{s}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{17,3\\}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $s$ is ${s}_{\\text{min}}=3,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $s(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
