    "q-344468": {
      "dom_id": "step-q344468",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14243229/assets/36a84f32380e5735b130f6b8fce26655738a0c86b210a732893dc8500d9cbbf8.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Given that $f(2)=-3,$ what is the minimum value ${f}_{\\text{min}}$ of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,5]?$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,2,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of $2$ trapezoids. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14243229/assets/cf8e3aa5a6000966c44b36071b2269540578cf123a19024a20a62fb61896dc51.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =-3 \\\\ f(-4) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(2)-{\\int }_{-4}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3-(-16) \\\\  & =13 \\\\ f(5) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3+4 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-3,13,1\\}=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-3,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=2.$",
